hey guys i'm kamna you can find me on  instagram at connorvospherin and i share  south asian inspired tips recipes and  hacks to make life easier some people  think indian cooking can be complicated  and i can understand why when you see  the recipe list it's usually this long  that's why today i'm gonna be making  three five ingredient indian desserts so  let's get started so the first recipe  i'm going to be showing you today is  mango kolpi mango kofi is a traditional  indian ice cream and what makes it  different than american ice cream is  that it's made without air so it has a  richer creamier denser taste to get  started we're going to bloom our saffron  saffron  is one of my favorite ingredients  because it's found in a lot of  traditional indian cooking and it's also  a really powerful antioxidant so i'm  going to put in about a pinch that's  about 10 to 15 strands and add in about  a tablespoon of hot water so adding a  tablespoon of hot water to your pinch of  saffron really helps extract that flavor  from the saffron and saffron can be a  little pricey so this gives you a little  bit more bang for your buck i'm gonna  set this aside for about five minutes  just to let these flavors really get  extracted now that my saffron is off to  the side i'm gonna show you how i crush  fresh cardamom and all i do is i take  the cardamom out of the pod  remove the shell and just make sure that  you get all the black seeds out put them  into your mortar and pestle and then you  just give it a nice pound  that looks great i'm going to set that  aside and now get started on our whipped  cream  so now i'm gonna add my heavy cream so  this has been chilled a bit and i'm  adding it to the bowl and i'm gonna whip  it until i get a nice soft peace  now i'm ready to add the rest of my  ingredients so first thing is half a can  of sweetened condensed milk you want to  just gently fold that in to  the heavy whipping cream now i'm adding  my mango caser so this is canned mango  pulp i get from the indian store you can  use fresh alfonso mango if you can find  it but if you use canned mango pulp  that's completely fine too and now  you'll see it turn that beautiful orange  color that's signature to mango cool add  in my ground cardamom and my saffron  with the water  get that a gentle nice mix  once you get this mixed and nice and  incorporated we're gonna add it to our  bowl it looks so beautiful love that  color that's exactly the color we were  looking for i'm gonna cover this and pop  it in the freezer and let this freeze  for about eight hours or overnight so  this is what the coffee should look like  coming out of the freezer it's gotten  nice and hard but it'll still have that  signature creamy texture now we're ready  to dig in and scoop it out and i love  seeing the saffron the cardamom and look  at that rich  orange mango color  and now i'm just going to sprinkle it  with a little bit of rose petal a little  bit of saffron just to give it a little  pretty look there we go mango coffee i  cannot wait to try this  that mango just brings back so many  memories of summers in india the mango  just shines in this recipe it goes so  well with the saffron and the cardamom  honestly i think this is the best batch  of coffee i've ever made so next we'll  be making coconut ladoos coconut ladoos  are like a little ball of joy we have  these for the bali the indian new year  good night's short these celebrations  and i also use coconut ladoos and  instead of holiday cookies i give these  to my friends during christmas and they  love them because they're just so  different and they taste so good so the  first step that we're gonna do is we're  gonna add desiccated coconut desiccated  coconut is coconut that has the air and  the moisture has been taken out of the  coconut and all that remains is the  spine powder now if you don't have  desiccated coconut you can just take  dried coconut flakes put them in the  food processor and get this nice  desiccated coconut powder i'm using an  insta pot right now you can use your  stove as well but what we're going to do  is we're going to add this to a little  bit of low heat to get these nice and  toasty the only thing you really have to  remember when you are putting in your  coconut is you just have to keep  stirring because you don't want any part  of it to burn did you can almost feel  that it's become a little bit crispier  and just turns a tiny bit of light brown  now we're gonna add our sweetened  condensed milk and you just put it right  in mix it right in remember to turn your  heat off  and you just mix this together now what  i love is you don't have to be too  gentle you really have to use muscle to  just get it all mixed in and you can  already see a dough forming  and now you're going to add your  cardamom it really just elevates this  dough wakes it up this recipe you don't  have to be too gentle you don't have to  worry about messing up and so there's  really nothing that can go wrong here  which is why i love it it's kind of like  my fail proof  dessert and see  now this is how the dough should look  the only thing is is that the dough  right now it's a little too soft so i'm  going to put this in the fridge for  about five minutes just so it gets  hardened and it's easier to work with  this is the dough out of the fridge and  you can see that it has hardened a lot  and now we're gonna do my favorite part  which is making our laddu balls so i  take about a golf ball size and i just  roll it in my hands i can feel it  sticking just a bit so i'm going to show  you guys a little hack i put a little  bit of ghee or a little bit of coconut  oil on my hands and that way the dough  doesn't stick to my hands i take this  ball here you don't have to make it  perfect just want to get it into a nice  little round ball and then this is what  i like to do i roll it in a bit of  shredded coconut just to give it a nice  beautiful little texture here and there  you go these are my ladies you can kind  of stack them and plate them how you  like you can see they have this  beautiful round shape but what i'm going  to do is i'm going to put them in the  fridge for another three three to five  minutes just so they harden up and they  really hold that shape and i'm going to  check back in and what i like to do is  at the very end i take a little bit of  crushed pistachio and i just sprinkle it  all over the top and there you go  coconut ladies with five ingredients  oh my gosh  and what i love is that these really  have that authentic indian taste people  will think you spent hours making these  but they were so easy to make for this  last recipe we're going to be making  saffron shrikhand mousse shrikhand is a  dish found in the western states of  india it's typically served at weddings  or you can even use shrikhand as a side  dish because it really complements kind  of spicy indian food where you want a  little bit of yogurt to cool it down so  first thing you have to remember to hang  your yogurt what you do is you take a  strainer and you put it over a bowl this  bowl is going to collect the excess  liquid or the whey from your yogurt and  then you take a cheesecloth or a piece  of muslin cloth and put it over your  strainer just like this and now you're  going to add your yogurt i'm going to  put that right into the cheesecloth next  we're going to wrap this up  and what you want to do is just create a  little ball and just give it a nice  little twist i can already see some of  the liquid or that whey is kind of  coming out and that's exactly what we  want i'm just going to let this sit in  the fridge for about six to eight hours  i like to do overnight that way i know  all that whey has separated from the  yogurt this is what the yogurt looks  like after you've kept it in the fridge  overnight and you can see that kind of  liquid that whey has drained out from  the yogurt you can keep the way if you  want to i know some people use this in  their smoothies i personally just  discard it but do whatever you like i  just love to see this part how it looks  oh that looks so beautiful look at that  it's a lot thicker a lot harder it  almost looks like a dough so i have my  hung curd and i put it right into my  bowl now i'm going to add one cup of  powdered sugar i've got my silk saffron  just like i did in my kulfi recipe we're  going to add it right to the mixture  with the water and the last thing is  we're going to add in our ground  cardamom and we're just going to mix  these together but you really do have to  use a little bit of mussel because that  yogurt is kind of stiff perfect this is  exactly how i want it to look at this  point it's pretty much done you can see  it has this really velvety nice creamy  texture with a little taste  perfect and now all you have to do is  transfer it to your serving dishes what  i like to do is i like to top with a  little whipped cream and crushed nuts at  the end this is optional but my nanny  always told me my grandma always told me  the best part about making indian  desserts is to make them your own  sprinkles sprinkle with just a little  bit of fresh nuts and there you go five  ingredients saffron shrikhand mousse  this tastes so silky you can taste the  cardamom that saffron really just  married so well together this tastes  like the perfect sunday afternoon  dessert and there you go three five  ingredient indian desserts first we made  our mango kofi then our coconut ladoos  and finally our saffron shrikhand mousse  i cannot wait for you guys to try this  and let me know what your favorite is in  the comments below  yay  oh yes  [Music]  [Applause]  you
 

